course: dictionary
lesson: 761
title: Beacon: a light that warns, guides, or gives hope
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A beacon is a light or fire that shows people where to go or warns them of danger. «People also used signals that travel by sight or sound — beacon fires on hilltops, smoke and drums — but these could carry only simple messages agreed in advance.» The clues are fires on hilltops and signals that travel by sight, so a beacon is something bright that people can see from far away.
explain: Beacons can also be machines. Some send out a radio signal so rescuers can find a lost walker or a boat. Ask yourself: is this a light, a signal, or a person? The words around the word tell you which kind of beacon it is.
explain: The word can also be used for a person. «The nurse was a beacon of hope for the frightened children.» Nobody is set on fire here. A beacon of hope is someone who shows others the way and makes them feel better. Test your meaning in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: A beacon is a light or fire on a hill, tower or coast that warns people or guides them.
fact: A beacon can also be a device that sends out a radio signal to show where something or someone is.
fact: «A beacon of hope» means a person or thing that gives others hope or shows the way.
fact: «In the UK zebra crossings have flashing orange globes on black and white poles, called Belisha beacons.» These flashing lights warn drivers that people may cross.
fact: Beacon fires on hilltops once passed warnings across the country, for example when the Spanish Armada came to England.
exercise: «People also used signals that travel by sight or sound — beacon fires on hilltops, smoke and drums — but these could carry only simple messages agreed in advance.» What is a beacon in this sentence? | *A light or fire on a hilltop that sends a message | A slow walk up a hill | A kind of smoke you can eat | A soft drum played at dinner
exercise: «In the UK zebra crossings have flashing orange globes on black and white poles, called Belisha beacons.» What are Belisha beacons? | Signs that show the speed limit | Black and white stripes painted on the road | *Flashing orange lights at zebra crossings | Tall towers for radio
exercise: «The nurse was a beacon of hope for the frightened children.» What does beacon mean here? | A radio signal from a boat | A bright cloud in the sky | *Someone who gives others hope and shows the way | A fire on a hill
test: Lost walkers can press an emergency beacon so rescuers can find them. What does the beacon do? | Calls their parents on the phone | Keeps the walkers warm | *Sends out a signal so rescuers can find them | Makes a map of the mountain
test: Which of these is a beacon that guides ships at night? | A pencil case | A cup of tea | A sandcastle | *A lighthouse
exam: «He was a beacon in the dark times, always cheering us up.» What does beacon mean in this sentence? | A kind of boat with sails | A loud drum played at a fair | *Someone or something that gives hope and shows the way | A type of cloud that floats low
